
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that keeps the scope honest.

  Next question, and it's forced by an awkward fact in this repo: there are two incompatible module worlds here. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  is a plain browser script loaded by [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m with no module system at all, while [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m uses
  CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m. There's no bundler and no test runner. A browser can't [38;5;153mrequire()[39m, so the preferences module can't
  simply live in [38;5;153msrc/[39m alongside [38;5;153mutils.js[39m and be used by [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the preferences module is loaded, which also decides whether it can be unit-tested outside a browser.

  - [1mPlain script + global[0m — add [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m, load it before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, expose one global. Matches [38;5;153mapp.js[39m exactly, no changes
    to how anything loads. Costs: a global, and testing needs a DOM-ish harness since the file isn't importable.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m exports, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Buys real imports, no
    global, and the same file is importable by a Node test with a [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m stub. Costs: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m both
    change, and [38;5;153mfile://[39m opening of the page stops working (module scripts need a server or the CORS rules bite).
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — normalizes everything. Costs: a dependency and build step for a four-file project. I'd call this
    over-engineering here.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m The testability difference is the deciding factor — a pure function over an injectable
  storage object is straightforward to test, and the global-script version isn't, which matters because I'll be
  proposing you add a test runner. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m caveat is the real cost; if you currently open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by
  double-clicking it, that breaks and the plain-script option becomes the better call.
